    "question_id": "Q1.6",
    "class": 12,
    "subject": "biology",
    "chapter_number": 1,
    "chapter_name": "reproduction in organisms",
    "question_type": "conceptual",
    "question_text": "Distinguish between asexual and sexual reproduction. Why is vegetative reproduction also considered as a type of asexual reproduction?",
    "question_latex": null,
    "mcq_options": [],
    "correct_option": null,
    "question_image": {
      "present": false,
      "description": null,
      "cloudinary_url": null,
      "latex_extracted": null,
      "source_page_number": null,
      "source_image_index": null,
      "ownership": "unknown"
    },
    "parts": [],
    "answer_text": "Asexual reproduction does not involve the fusion of the male and female gamete. It requires only one individual. The individuals produced are identical to the parent and are hence, called clones. In contrast, sexual reproduction involves the fusion of the male and female gamete of two individuals, typically one of each sex. The individuals produced are not identical to their parents and show variations from each other. Vegetative reproduction is considered a type of asexual reproduction because it involves the propagation of plants through certain vegetative parts without the production of seeds or spores.",
    "answer_latex": null,
    "steps": [],
    "final_answer": "Thus, vegetative reproduction is a form of asexual reproduction due to its reliance on vegetative parts.",
    "answer_image": {
      "present": false,
      "description": null,
      "cloudinary_url": null,
      "latex_extracted": null,
      "source_page_number": null,
      "source_image_index": null,
