    "chapter_number": 1,
    "chapter_name": "reproduction in organisms",
    "question_type": "conceptual",
    "question_text": "Why are offspring of oviparous animals at a greater risk as compared to offspring of viviparous animals?",
    "question_latex": null,
    "mcq_options": [],
    "correct_option": null,
    "question_image": {
      "present": false,
      "description": null,
      "cloudinary_url": null,
      "latex_extracted": null,
      "source_page_number": null,
      "source_image_index": null,
      "ownership": "unknown"
    },
    "parts": [],
    "answer_text": "Oviparous animals lay eggs outside their body. As a result, the eggs of these animals are under continuous threat from various environmental factors. On the other hand, in viviparous animals, the development of the egg takes place inside the body of the female. Hence, the offspring of an egg-laying or oviparous animal is at greater risk as compared to the offspring of a viviparous animal, which gives birth to its young ones.",
    "answer_latex": null,
    "steps": [],
    "final_answer": "Therefore, oviparous offspring are at greater risk due to external environmental threats compared to viviparous offspring.",
    "answer_image": {
      "present": false,
